  "name": "Multimodal_CSI_Text",
  "url": "https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/signalz/multimodal-csi-text/versions/1",
  "description": "",
  "rai:dataLimitations": "CSI-TextBench uses template-generated text descriptions derived from categorical labels rather than naturally co-occurring language. While this provides clean, reliable, and semantically consistent supervision, it bounds linguistic diversity compared to free-form captions. The dataset retains only CSI amplitude (phase is discarded due to device-dependent noise), which has been shown sufficient to achieve strong alignment results across all seven tasks. Open-vocabulary recognition generalizes well within everyday language but may degrade for highly specialized terminology outside the text encoder's pretraining distribution; anchored prompting substantially recovers accuracy in these cases. For SignFi, preprocessed CSI samples are not directly included due to its license; instead, we provide preprocessing scripts and CSI-text registry files so users can reproduce the full dataset from the publicly available original. These design choices offer a clean and extensible foundation for future work on richer text supervision and domain-adapted language encoders.",
  "rai:dataBiases": "CSI-TextBench combines three complementary source benchmarks that together span a broad range of environments, hardware platforms, and semantic granularity. CSI-Bench contributes real-world, in-the-wild data from 16 commercial WiFi device types, while XRF55 adds controlled recordings from 55 participants and SignFi provides high-granularity gesture data (276 classes). The multitask subset involves 7 users, which may underrepresent the full spectrum of demographic and behavioral diversity. Text descriptions are hand-written English-only templates; while this ensures label fidelity and semantic consistency, it introduces linguistic uniformity. To mitigate hardware-specific biases, text is deliberately grounded only in physical phenomena (activity, gesture, proximity) and excludes deployment artifacts such as device type or environment identity. The dataset's diversity across devices, environments, and task complexity provides a strong foundation, and future extensions can broaden subject pools and language coverage.",
  "rai:personalSensitiveInformation": "WiFi CSI captures radio-frequency channel amplitude measurements and contains no identifiable visual, audio, or biometric content, offering a structural privacy advantage over camera- and microphone-based sensing. The user identification task distinguishes individuals through behavioral motion signatures embedded in CSI patterns, not through personally identifiable biometric data. All source datasets (CSI-Bench, XRF55, SignFi) are publicly available and were collected under their respective Institutional Review Board (IRB) approvals. The released dataset contains preprocessed CSI amplitude tensors stored as HDF5 files and templated text descriptions; no raw personal data, names, images, or audio recordings are included. Users are identified only by anonymous numerical IDs (e.g., \"User 1\" through \"User 22\").",
  "rai:dataUseCases": "CSI-TextBench is designed for research on multimodal alignment between WiFi CSI signals and natural language. Primary use cases include: (1) zero-shot WiFi-based activity recognition, fall detection, motion source identification, proximity estimation, user identification, and sign/action recognition without class labels; (2) few-shot transfer to unseen classes using frozen CSI-text embeddings; (3) open-vocabulary recognition accepting arbitrary natural-language queries at inference; and (4) compositional ambient scene understanding that jointly resolves activity, proximity, and user identity from a single WiFi signal. Downstream applications span assistive living (elderly fall detection, respiration monitoring), smart building automation, and ambient intelligence systems. The benchmark also serves as a testbed for evaluating diverse alignment paradigms (contrastive, anchor-based, non-contrastive, reconstructive) under dense many-to-many signal-text correspondence.",
  "rai:dataSocialImpact": "CSI-TextBench enables privacy-preserving indoor contextual awareness using ubiquitous WiFi infrastructure already present in most environments, without requiring cameras or microphones. This supports socially beneficial applications such as elderly care, fall detection, and smart-home automation for aging populations and individuals with disabilities. By turning fixed-label sensors into queryable, composable perception layers, the dataset lowers the integration barrier for ambient intelligence agents. The same methodology transfers directly to other ambient modalities lacking natural text, including radar, mmWave, and acoustic sensing. However, as with any technology that detects human presence and activity, potential for misuse exists in unauthorized surveillance or tracking. The authors recommend explicit consent mechanisms, on-device processing, and access controls for any deployment of ambient sensing systems built on this work.",
  "rai:hasSyntheticData": false,
